        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): preserve ANSI sanitizer state across bash chunks",
        "body": "Closes #103668\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen a bash process splits ANSI/OSC controls across stdout callbacks, per-chunk stateless stripping can leak control-sequence fragments into streamed tool output, final results, and spill files.\n\nThis adds a reusable incremental ANSI stripper to terminal-core and keeps one sanitizer alive for each bash command. One-shot `stripAnsiSequences()` behavior stays unchanged while sanitization becomes invariant to callback chunking.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact rebased head: `01262f99db9cac554df4b0dd977e8c73510d5b0b`\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs packages/terminal-core/src/ansi.test.ts src/agents/sessions/tools/output-accumulator.test.ts src/agents/sessions/bash-executor.test.ts` passed: 3 files / 51 tests across 2 shards.\n- `node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs` against all changed terminal-core, accumulator, and bash files passed.\n- `oxfmt --check --threads=1` against all changed files passed.\n- `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passed.\n\nCoverage includes split ESC/C1 OSC and CSI controls, trailing ESC before OSC, bounded unterminated controls, accumulator flushing, and the real `executeBashWithOperations()` child-process path. The latter verifies clean streamed chunks and final `ABCDEFGH`, including sanitized spill-file output for large split OSC payloads.\n",
        "branch": "fix/103668-stateful-ansi-sanitizer",
        "head_sha": "01262f99db9cac554df4b0dd977e8c73510d5b0b",
        "author": "jincheng-xydt",
